97</fpage><lpage>110</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15040187</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1089/153871303766275763</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Robinson</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Newstetter</surname><given-names>WC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Uncertain science and certain deadlines: CDC responses to the media during the anthrax attacks of 2001</article-title><source>J Health Commun</source><year>2003</year><volume>8 Suppl 1</volume><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>34; discussion 148-51</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14692570</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1080/10810730305697</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Institute of Medicine. The Future of Public Health in the 21st Century.</article-title></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Becker</surname><given-names>LB</given-names></name><name><surname>Lowrey</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Claussen</surname><given-names>DS</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Why does the beat go on?</article-title><source>Newspaper Res J</source><year>2000</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>2</fpage><lpage>16</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>A journalist's guide to covering bioterrorism</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.rtndf.org/resources/bioterror.shtml"/></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hobbs</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kittler</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Fox</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Middleton</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Bates</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Communicating health information to an alarmed public facing a threat such as a bioterrorist attack</article-title><source>J Health Commun</source><year>2004</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>75</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14761834</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dunwoody</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Ryan</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Public information persons as mediators between scientists and journalists</article-title><source>Journalism Quarterly</source><year>1983</year><volume>60</volume><fpage>647</fpage><lpage>656</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ankney</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Curtin</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Delineating (and delimiting) the boundary spanning role of the medical public information officer</article-title><source>Public Relations Rev</source><year>2002</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>229</fpage><lpage>241</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0363-8111(02)00129-7</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Leichty</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Springston</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Elaborating public relations roles</article-title><source>Journal Mass Commun Q</source><year>1996</year><volume>73</volume><fpage>467</fpage><lpage>477</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wray</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Jupka</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>What does the public want to know in the event of a terrorist attack using plague?</article-title><source>Biosecur Bioterror</source><year>2004</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>208</fpage><lpage>215</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15588059</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1089/bsp.2004.2.208</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Perry</surname><given-names>RW</given-names></name><name><surname>Lindell</surname><given-names>MK</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Preparedness for emergency response: guidelines for the emergency planning process</article-title><source>Disasters</source><year>2003</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>336</fpage><lpage>350</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14725091</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.0361-3666.2003.00237.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ethiel</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><source>Terrorism: Informing the public</source><year>2002</year><publisher-name>Chicago, IL, McCormick Tribune Foundation</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>